---
title: First Day Using NAHPU
sidebar:
    label: NAHPU Day 1
    order: 2
---

This guide introduces NAHPU and gives you a safe place to practice before collection work. You do not need to be in the field or have a real specimen. NAHPU runs on any screen, though a laptop or tablet offers more room while you learn the layout. Use a clearly named practice project and sample values so exercise records stay separate from research data.

## Key concepts

{% recordmap label="How a paper field catalog maps to NAHPU: a project holds narratives, sites, events, and specimens, while personnel and taxa are shared across projects." hint="Select a record or the paper catalog to compare how they store field data." %}
{% recordnode id="paper" title="Paper catalog" summary="Notebook & ledger" detail="Combines handwritten journal pages with a numbered specimen ledger where locality, date, and collector are rewritten on every row. NAHPU records each place and sampling session once and links specimens to them." paper="Paper catalog: one bound volume where copying the same site and date onto every row takes time and invites transcription errors." /%}
{% recordnode id="project" title="Project" summary="One body of work" detail="Groups an expedition, a monitoring program, or laboratory preparation work. Most day-to-day screens and exports operate on the active project." paper="Paper catalog: one bound field catalog volume or expedition binder." /%}
{% recordnode id="narrative" title="Narrative" summary="Field journal" detail="Dated prose entries tied to a site and writer for daily observations, habitat context, and notes that do not fit structured specimen or event fields." paper="Paper catalog: the narrative journal section written alongside the specimen ledger." /%}
{% recordnode id="site" title="Site" summary="A place to revisit" detail="A place you can visit again, such as a camp or trap line. Enter its geography, habitat, and one or more coordinates once and reuse it across visits." paper="Paper catalog: the locality header rewritten at the top of pages or repeated with ditto marks on every row." /%}
{% recordnode id="event" title="Event" summary="Work at a site" detail="One sampling session at a site during a defined period: activity, collection methods, participants, effort, and conditions. A site can have several events." paper="Paper catalog: dates, collectors, trap effort, and weather repeated across ledger columns or scattered in journal prose." /%}
{% recordnode id="specimen" title="Specimen" summary="Linked to an event" detail="Links to one event, which supplies its site and sampling context. Carries the working Field ID from its physical label plus an internal UUID. Several specimens can share one event." paper="Paper catalog: one numbered line in the specimen ledger paired with a handwritten physical tag." /%}
{% recordnode id="personnel" title="Personnel" summary="Shared" detail="People are stored in the installation database and shared across projects. Editing a person updates every project that references that person." paper="Paper catalog: collector names, initials, and personal number series written anew in each notebook." /%}
{% recordnode id="taxa" title="Taxa" summary="Shared" detail="The taxon registry is shared across projects. To correct a single identification, update the specimen record rather than editing the shared taxon." paper="Paper catalog: species names written on each row and crossed out in ink when re-identified." /%}
{% /recordmap %}

### From paper catalog to linked records

In a paper field catalog, you rewrite the same locality, date, and collector across ledger rows. NAHPU separates those layers so you enter each context once:

- **Project** sets the boundary for how you manage and export one expedition, monitoring program, or lab batch.
- **Site → Event → Specimen** links where you worked (`Site`), when and how you sampled (`Event`), and what you collected (`Specimen`). Use one site for a revisited trap line, separate events for each sampling date or session, and link all specimens from a session to that event. Keep collection dates distinct from laboratory preparation dates.
- **Narrative** holds your prose field journal for a site and date without crowding specimen fields.
- **Personnel and Taxa** live in the database across projects. Editing a shared person or taxon affects every project that uses it; to fix one misidentified specimen, change the specimen's identification, not the shared taxon entry.

### Location, locality, and coordinates

Project location describes the broad study area. Site geography records the administrative hierarchy and specific locality description, which can be shared across sites, while Site IDs distinguish individual places such as a camp or trap line.

A single site can store several coordinates, such as one per trap or net. Select the specific coordinate on a specimen when known. **Coordinate uncertainty** records GPS accuracy for the point, whereas **specimen coordinate extent** on the specimen form records how far the organism could have been from that point.

### Field IDs and UUIDs

A **Field ID** is the working identifier written on a physical specimen tag:

- **Personnel Field IDs** combine a Cataloger's initials and personal number series. They reduce the risk of duplicate numbers when multiple teams prepare and catalog specimens in parallel.
- **Project Field IDs** use a project prefix, sequential catalog number, and suffix. They suit assembly-line preparation where one person catalogs for the whole project.

NAHPU is [FAIR](https://www.go-fair.org/fair-principles/) by design and automatically assigns internal UUIDs to specimen and symbiont records (and optionally to each specimen part), independent of Field IDs.

### Roles and taxon identification

NAHPU separates **specimen care roles** from **event personnel roles**:

- **Specimen care roles** are set when creating a person and govern specimen fields: a `Cataloger` records the specimen (highest rank, eligible for all personnel fields), a `Preparator` prepares it, and a `Determiner` identifies it.
- **Event personnel roles** describe participation in a specific collecting event (such as leader, student helper, local guide, or driver) and can be customized per event.

The taxon registry defaults to species imports but accepts higher ranks such as genus or family, so you can record an identification at the rank you can support and log its **identification confidence**.

### Settings, vocabularies, and custom fields

Controlled vocabularies populate dropdown lists such as collection methods and preparation types; share user configurations so a team uses identical terms. The active catalog format selects the form fields for new specimens on the device, and one project can hold multiple taxon groups. Custom fields capture project-scoped or installation-wide observations that do not fit built-in fields.

The **project time zone** documents the local time used for recorded observations. It serves as an interpretive reference and never alters the device clock or converts existing timestamps.

### Saving, attachments, and backups

Most form fields save as you edit, while dialogs, imports, and exports require confirmation. Use **Narratives** for prose context, **Associated Data** for links and non-media files (links do not store offline copies), and **Media** for images, audio, or video.

Each export format serves a distinct purpose:

- **Project identity**: shares only the project UUID so team devices can merge records later.
- **Project transfer** (`.zip` / `.tar.gz`, or light `.json.gz` without media): moves project records and attachments between devices.
- **User configuration**: shares vocabularies, templates, and presets.
- **Database backup**: creates an installation-wide recovery point. Tabular exports and PDFs are for review and printing, not backups.

## Practice using NAHPU

{% fieldtrail label="Practice trail with one waypoint for each practice step." hint="Select a waypoint to jump to its step. Visited steps are checked." progress="{visited} of {total} steps visited" /%}

{% steps %}

1. **Open NAHPU and explore setup**

    Open `Setup NAHPU` from the home screen or drawer. Inspect the catalog format, identifier modes, controlled vocabularies, and export presets, or import a team configuration. Locate the dashboard, navigation bar, and Info buttons. See [Settings](https://nahpu.app/en/usages/settings/).

2. **Create a practice project**

    Select `Create project` > `Create new project` and name it `NAHPU Practice`. Enter project details and the reference time zone, choose a catalog format and Field ID mode, and finish the review. For an existing team project, import its project identity to share its UUID. See [Projects](https://nahpu.app/en/usages/projects/).

3. **Add practice personnel and taxa**

    Add personnel, assign their specimen care roles, and coordinate initials and starting numbers across devices. Register taxa manually by rank or import a CSV, TSV, or Excel file and verify the column mapping (select a shared class if the `Class` column is omitted). Review proposed ranks before confirming file or QR imports. See [Personnel](https://nahpu.app/en/usages/personnel/) and [Taxon Registry](https://nahpu.app/en/usages/taxon/).

4. **Create a practice site**

    Open Sites and select `+`. Enter a stable Site ID, administrative geography, and habitat description. Add one or more coordinates manually, from device GPS, from a file, or via QR, then verify the map, datum, units, and uncertainty. Reuse this site for repeat visits. See [Sites](https://nahpu.app/en/usages/sites/).

5. **Add a practice event**

    Open Events and select `+`. Pick your practice site, enter start and end dates and times, and add an Event ID suffix if multiple sessions occur at the same site on one date. Record the activity, collection methods, event roles, weather, and astronomy values. Duplicating an event copies its setup while leaving environmental measurements blank. See [Collecting Events](https://nahpu.app/en/usages/events/).

6. **Create a sample specimen record**

    Open Specimen Records and select `+` using a practice label. Verify the Field ID against the label, assign the Cataloger, Preparator, and Determiner, and choose the taxon and identification confidence (use a higher rank if species is uncertain). Link the collecting event, enter capture details, measurements, parts, and any symbiont records, and attach sample Media or Associated Data. Reopen the record to confirm what was saved. See [Specimen Records](https://nahpu.app/en/usages/specimens/).

7. **Review records and write a narrative**

    Cross-check Field IDs, counts, linked events, coordinates, and attachments against your practice labels. Open Narratives to write a short field journal entry for the site and preview the rendered Markdown. Try `Export records` for a review table and `Export documents` for printable labels or catalogs. See [Narratives](https://nahpu.app/en/usages/narrative/) and [Export](https://nahpu.app/en/usages/export/).

8. **Practice project transfer and recovery**

    Export the practice project as a `.zip` or `.tar.gz` bundle (or light `.json.gz` without media) and inspect any warnings. If you have a second device, test `Import project` from the home screen, or `Merge project` when the project UUID already exists there. Always create a database backup before importing or merging, and never overwrite a live research database for practice. See [Project transfer and recovery](https://nahpu.app/en/usages/export/project-transfer/).

{% /steps %}

## Check your understanding

Before starting field or lab work, confirm that you can explain how NAHPU replaces repeated paper catalog columns with the site–event–specimen link, tell which Field ID mode your project uses, correct one specimen's identification without altering the shared taxon, and choose between sharing project identity, transferring a project bundle, and backing up the database.

- [Configure custom fields](https://nahpu.app/en/how-to-recipes/prepare/configure-custom-fields/)
- [Browse the Cookbook](https://nahpu.app/en/how-to-recipes/)
